Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must have a **written treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved within the duration of the plan**;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the plan.  
  [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 3.2.1.6 (effective 2/1/2025; current)

## Documentation requirement
In addition to having the plan, the provider’s record must include **a copy of the treatment plan** as part of the documentation for services. The record must also include service notes for each billed date of service showing the skilled intervention, outcome/beneficiary response, date of service, duration in minutes, and the signature/credentials of the person providing the service.  
[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 7.2 (effective 2/1/2025; current)

## Bottom line
So, the **plan of care requirement** is not just that a treatment plan exist; it must contain the specific elements listed in **§ 3.2.1.6**, be limited to a **maximum six-month period**, and be **signed and dated by the professional on or before the plan start date**. The provider must also keep **a copy of that treatment plan** in the medical record.  
[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) §§ 3.2.1.6, 7.2

I checked amendments, and the current authoritative document remains **Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**.

NC’s CAP/DA waiver is a **home- and community-based services waiver under section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, implemented through the federal HCBS waiver regulations in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. The NC CAP/DA policy itself describes CAP/DA as a waiver program for disabled adults under NC Medicaid, but the federal waiver authority and application requirements come from the federal regulations rather than the state clinical policy. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441); [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## What the state must demonstrate in its waiver application

For a 1915(c) waiver request, the state must submit the **assurances** required by federal law and the **supporting documentation** that proves those assurances. Specifically:

### 1. The waiver request must include the required waiver contents
Under **42 CFR § 441.301**, the request must include:

- the **assurances** required by § 441.302 and the **supporting documentation** required by § 441.303;
- any requested waivers of statewideness, comparability, or certain income/resource rules when applicable;
- a statement about whether the state will refuse HCBS when expected waiver costs would exceed institutional costs;
- that services will be provided under a **written person-centered service plan** approved by the Medicaid agency;
- that services will be furnished only to people who, without waiver services, would require the Medicaid-covered level of care in a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID, as applicable;
- the qualifications of the people who develop the plan of care;
- the target group to be served; and
- separate definitions of each waiver service. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 2. The state must give CMS satisfactory “assurances”
Under **42 CFR § 441.302**, CMS will not grant the waiver unless the Medicaid agency gives satisfactory assurances, including at least:

- **health and welfare safeguards** for beneficiaries;
- adequate provider standards and compliance with licensure/certification rules;
- **financial accountability** for waiver funds;
- procedures showing that beneficiaries are informed of feasible alternatives and may choose between institutional care and HCBS;
- that each beneficiary receives needed services under an adequate plan of care/person-centered service plan;
- that the state has an appropriate system for **evaluation and reevaluation** of level of care and need;
- that waiver services are furnished in **home and community-based settings** as required by the regulations; and
- current rules also require an **incident management system** that identifies, reports, investigates, resolves, and trends critical incidents. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 3. The state must provide supporting documentation for those assurances
Under **42 CFR § 441.303**, the state must provide enough information to support the assurances, including:

- a description of safeguards protecting beneficiaries’ health and welfare;
- records and systems supporting **financial accountability**;
- its plan for **evaluation and reevaluation**, including:
  - who performs evaluations,
  - how evaluations are performed,
  - the evaluation form used,
  - procedures for maintaining written documentation, and
  - procedures for periodic reevaluation;
- how the state will inform eligible beneficiaries about alternatives and permit a choice between institutional services and HCBS;
- how post-eligibility income/resource rules will be applied when relevant; and
- documentation supporting **cost neutrality**. The regulation requires the state to show that the **average per capita Medicaid cost of waiver services plus other Medicaid services for waiver participants will not exceed the average per capita Medicaid cost of institutional and other Medicaid services that would have been provided without the waiver**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Public-notice requirement before submission
If the state is submitting a demonstration application or extension to CMS, **42 CFR § 431.408** requires at least a **30-day public notice and comment period** and public hearings with specified content in the notice. That provision is a federal procedural requirement for those submissions. [42 CFR Part 431](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-431)

## NC CAP/DA policy context
NC’s current CAP/DA clinical policy is effective **September 1, 2025**, and the amendment check in the policy corpus shows it as the authoritative current policy document. The retrieved state-policy text confirms CAP/DA is an NC Medicaid program with beneficiary eligibility and coverage criteria, but the federal waiver authority and the required contents of the waiver application are established in **42 CFR Part 441**, not in the CAP/DA clinical policy text retrieved here. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Bottom line
So, the short answer is:

- **Federal statutory authority:** section **1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, implemented in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**.  
- **What NC must demonstrate:** satisfactory CMS assurances and supporting documentation on **health and welfare, provider standards, person-centered planning, level-of-care eligibility, beneficiary choice, evaluation/reevaluation, financial accountability, and cost neutrality**, plus any required public-notice procedures applicable to the submission. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441); [42 CFR Part 431](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-431)

