NC Medicaid requires HIV case management providers to meet both **initial certification standards** and **ongoing recertification/operational requirements**. A provider can be **decertified** if it fails those standards or commits specified violations.

## Certification requirements

Under Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, an HIV case management provider must:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure services are provided by **qualified case managers** and supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s staff criteria;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- show compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary, operational, and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days** and actual changes within **five business days**; and
- within **one year of Medicaid enrollment**, obtain **national accreditation** from one of the designated accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.1 (effective Jan. 15, 2020; no amendments returned)

The policy’s application requirements also show what NC Medicaid expects for certification and recertification operations. These include, among other things:

- a **physical business site** verified by site visit, not in a private residence or vehicle;
- organizational and ownership information;
- access to required web-based applications;
- a business plan and financial information;
- computer capability for electronic records, HIPAA compliance, data analysis, and electronic billing;
- compliance with a **precertification onsite visit**;
- compliance with all applicable **licensure and certification requirements**; and
- written agency policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, electronic records, medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) administrative requirements section (effective Jan. 15, 2020; no amendments returned)

For recertification specifically, the HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist requires submission showing, among other things:

- all case managers and supervisors with dates of employment;
- all counties served;
- all services provided;
- operating hours and an **after-hours emergency plan**;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction-survey frequency and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients.  
  [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current document; no amendments returned)

## When a provider may be decertified

A provider may be decertified for any of the following reasons listed in Policy 12B § 6.2.5:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing** practices;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of beneficiary **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do **not meet policy qualifications**;
- staff failure to complete mandatory basic training within **90 days** of employment;
- staff failure to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within required time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to **support billing**;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of key agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in **§ 6.2**.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.5 (effective Jan. 15, 2020; no amendments returned)

## Related compliance points that matter in practice

NC Medicaid also monitors providers through **quality assurance site visits**, including:

- review of staff qualifications and training;
- chart review of active and closed caseloads;
- comparison of billing records to documentation;
- investigation of complaints; and
- review of quality improvement activities.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 7.8 (effective Jan. 15, 2020; no amendments returned)

And because inadequate documentation is an express ground for decertification, the documentation rules in §§ 7.5 and 7.6 are important. They require, among other things, timely assessments, care plans, progress notes, physician orders supporting medical necessity, quarterly updates, and records sufficient to support claims.  
[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) §§ 7.5, 7.6 (effective Jan. 15, 2020; no amendments returned)

## Bottom line

To stay certified, an HIV case management provider must maintain qualified staff and supervision, site enrollment, accreditation, compliance systems, documentation, and quality improvement processes. NC Medicaid may decertify the provider for failures in service delivery, staffing, training, documentation, billing integrity, confidentiality, corrective action, legal compliance, or maintenance of certification standards.

